// Live-action genre: corporate real-estate thriller. Strong-band craft calibration.
// Author: STORYMACHINE benchmark contributor, 2026.

INT. VANCE DEVELOPMENT - 38TH FLOOR CONFERENCE ROOM - NIGHT

Floor-to-ceiling glass over a city skyline gone dark except for the grid of streetlights below. A single TERM SHEET lies in the center of a twenty-seat table. WREN VANCE, 30s, blazer off, sleeves rolled, slides it toward a rival's empty chair.

WREN
They'll counter at six in the morning when their counsel lands. That gives us nine hours to find whatever they've buried in the boilerplate before we sign away the anchor deal of this firm.

She stacks the exhibits: surveys, environmental reports, a title abstract bound with a cloth tie. Her phone buzzes on the glass. A text from an unknown number, no signature.

She picks it up and reads the screen.

WREN
(aloud)
"The building has a second deed. Check the courthouse before they do."

She stares at it. The sender ID is blank. She screenshots it, then locks the phone.

INT. VANCE DEVELOPMENT - WREN'S OFFICE - LATER

CLAIRE ADEYEMI, 50s, Wren's mentor and co-founder, reads the anonymous text on Wren's phone. Her face tightens around the mouth before she can school it.

CLAIRE
You shouldn't have shown me this. I was on the original acquisition team for the Hartwell parcel. If there's a second deed out there, my name is somewhere in the chain.

WREN
Then help me find it before sunrise. The truth is, I came to you because I trust your read of these filings. You built this title practice.

CLAIRE
The truth is, I've been dreading the day you'd ask. I signed off on a transfer I never fully understood, fourteen years ago, when you were still in grad school and I was trying to land the firm's first major deal.

WREN
What didn't you understand?

CLAIRE
The release. The original owner was supposed to file a release of an older deed as a condition of close. I have no memory of confirming it cleared. I assumed it had, because the deal closed, and then I never looked back.

INT. DOWNTOWN COURTHOUSE - STEPS - PRE-DAWN

Wren jogs up the marble steps, coffee in one hand, phone in the other. The brass doors are locked, a laminated sign reading HOURS 8:00 - 4:30 WEEKDAYS.

WREN
(to herself, breath fogging)
Closes at eight. Nine hours just became six, and Hartwell's counsel lands at six sharp.

She tries the door anyway, pressing her face to the glass. Inside, the lobby is dark. She steps back and dials.

INT. COUNTY RECORDS - TITLE DIVISION - MORNING

A fluorescent-lit basement room of microfiche readers and vinyl-bound plat books. Claire works the reader beside Wren, feeding reel after reel of scanned deeds. Claire's hand trembles as she turns the crank wheel.

CLAIRE
There. May fourteenth, twelve years ago. The second deed. Filed the same week I countersigned the transfer. The original owner never released it.

WREN
Which means Hartwell never owned the land free and clear when he sold it to us, and he never owned it free and clear when he sold it to the three buyers before us. He's been passing an unresolved claim down the line.

CLAIRE
The truth is, I should have caught this then. I was protecting the firm's first big deal, and I was protecting you from seeing me fail at the one thing I was supposed to be good at.

WREN
The truth is, I would rather have known. You taught me to read title, Claire, and you can stop protecting me from the consequences of your own lesson.

INT. VANCE DEVELOPMENT - JUNIOR ASSOCIATE'S DESK - DAY

Wren hands the second deed and the plat map to JUNIOR ASSOCIATE LEO, 20s, who logs the chain of title into a spreadsheet, tracing each transfer backward.

LEO
Twelve years of resales, and the unreleased deed traveled with every one of them. Hartwell's name first appears four years ago, but the break in the chain is the original transfer Claire countersigned.

WREN
Can we trace the original owner?

LEO
The deed lists a family trust, dissolved. But the trustee of record is still listed in the state registry. I can have an address by lunch.

WREN
Get it. We're not buying from Hartwell. We're buying from the person who actually holds title.

EXT. SUBURBAN BUNGALOW - DAY

Wren knocks on the door of a small house with a ramp to the porch. MRS. ADEBAYO, 80s, opens it, leaning on a cane.

WREN
Mrs. Adebayo? My name is Wren Vance. I'm with Vance Development. May I ask you about a deed your late husband's trust held on the downtown parcel?

MRS. ADEBAYO
I wondered when someone would come. He tried to release it years ago and the paperwork came back wrong. He passed before we sorted it.

WREN
Then it was never his fault the release didn't clear. And it's not yours now. I'd like to make you a fair offer, clean, with proper counsel, and to put the title right before anyone else gets hurt by it.

MRS. ADEBAYO
(studying her)
You're not like the others who've called about that lot.

WREN
No, ma'am. I'm trying not to be.

INT. HARTWELL TOWER - LOBBY - DAY

Wren meets the rival developer, MARCUS HARTWELL, 60s, beside a construction hoarding where marble meets raw concrete. He holds out a fresh term sheet, already countersigned on his side, the price bumped.

MARCUS
Final offer, Ms. Vance. Midnight tonight, hard deadline, or we break ground on the new schedule with or without your participation.

WREN
You're in a hurry for a seller with a clean parcel. Buildings with clean title don't need a midnight deadline.

MARCUS
Buildings with a motivated seller do. Take the number, close tonight, everyone goes home happy.

WREN
I don't go home happy when I don't understand the rush. I'll see you at midnight, Mr. Hartwell. With questions.

She does not take the sheet. Hartwell's smile thins at the corners.

INT. VANCE DEVELOPMENT - WREN'S OFFICE - NIGHT

Wren pins the second deed beside the term sheet and the title abstract on the glass wall, connecting them with dry-erase lines. Claire stands across the desk, arms folded.

WREN
Hartwell's counter was a trap. He wanted us to close before midnight so the broken title became our problem the moment we signed. He's done it before. We need to walk away and buy the parcel from the original owner directly.

CLAIRE
We walk away, we lose the anchor deal the firm has spent eighteen months building, and the partners will ask why. The truth is, I built half this company on that original mistake, and I've been afraid it would surface since the day you made partner.

WREN
Then we build the other half honestly. Midnight is the deadline they set, and we let it pass on our terms, not theirs.

CLAIRE
And the original owner?

WREN
We find them at the courthouse at eight a.m. and we make a clean offer. No more inherited secrets.

INT. HARTWELL TOWER - PENTHOUSE - NIGHT

Wren lays the second deed, the plat map, and the unreleased transfer on Marcus Hartwell's desk under his reading lamp. He goes very still.

WREN
You sold us a building you did not own. The filing is right here, dated twelve years ago, and here is the original owner's release, which you never obtained and which never cleared.

MARCUS
This deal closes tonight, Ms. Vance, or it never closes. Those are your only options.

WREN
You're wrong. There's a third option, and it's the one I came up here to take.

She lifts the papers off his desk and squares them into her bag.

MARCUS
(after her, sharpening)
You walk out of this office, you don't come back. The price resets in the morning.

WREN
(at the door)
Good. Then we both know where we stand at midnight.

INT. VANCE DEVELOPMENT - 38TH FLOOR CONFERENCE ROOM - MIDNIGHT

The clock on the wall ticks past midnight. The term sheet is untouched in the center of the table. Claire picks it up, holds it a moment, then feeds it into the shredder by the credenza. The machine chews it to confetti.

CLAIRE
We let the deadline pass. Hartwell's trap collapses on him, and the firm takes the reputational hit standing up.

WREN
We take the second deed to the original owner at eight a.m. and we buy the title clean, at a fair number, with no more inherited secrets.

CLAIRE
The truth is, I'm grateful you found the courage I didn't have back then. I forgive myself, finally, for the fourteen years I spent waiting for the other shoe to drop.

WREN
And I'm grateful you trusted me with the truth tonight instead of protecting me from it. We start over in the morning, Claire. With one building we actually own.

Claire switches off the conference-room lights. The skyline glows beyond the glass, the deadline past and the firm still standing, the shredder quiet at last.

FADE OUT.
